it's a story as old as time  change is the only constant and change  is hard  after all we're only human  the change that is rolling out new  versions of your software is no  exception to this rule  even with all of the improvements  containers and kubernetes have brought  to the table  in a previous video linked below we  discussed how canary deployments in gke  can make this change for your software  easier  today let's demonstrate those  foundations and walk through the  different ways canary deployments in gke  can work  [Music]  my name is anthony bouchon developer  relations engineer here at google  in this walkthrough we'll show the  fundamentals to implicit and explicit  approaches to rolling out a canary  deployment in gke  what is out of scope for this demo is  orchestrating canary deployments as part  of an end-to-end pipeline in platforms  like cloud build or cloud deploy  want us to dive into that in the future  let us know in the comments and  subscribe to get updates  or are you still new to the overall  concept of canary deployments  check out our concepts video in the  description and come back after with  that said let's get right to it  we'll start with a single gke cluster  called prod  where we will deploy a python now  this app's only job in this world is to  tell us what version it is  i know very important  the app uses flask to return a message  that pulls its release version from an  environment variable named release using  the python os module  if we switch over to the kubernetes  manifest we can see that this  environment variable gets its value from  the pod label that references the  release version this environment  variable is set via field ref starting  in line 23 of this manifest the value of  this environment variable  will be surfaced in the responses that  different versions of our apps serve  first we have a stable release of our  app that has the appropriate release as  the key and stable as the value label  in kind  the canary release of our app will have  the appropriate release as the key  canary as the value label  it's important to note that each of  these are separate kubernetes  deployments  however they do share a common label  that allows for them to be selected by  the same kubernetes service  this means users or other clients can  access both versions of our app using  the same endpoint to be specific our  kubernetes service will route traffic to  pods with the label app name as the key  and app as the value regardless of their  differing release labels  because we are using a kubernetes  service and are using cube proxy in this  example  a pod behind the service will be chosen  at random  so let's deploy the stable and canary  version of our app and see what happens  let's set the scene first  using coupe ctx we can see that our  current cluster is the prod cluster we  showed earlier  using cubens we can see that we're  deploying all of this to the same app  namespace  and the warning you see  wouldn't worry about it for this demo  but it's a nice reminder to update your  tooling to use the out of tree gke auth  plugin  anyways using coupe ctl we'll start by  deploying the stable version of the  application starting with a single  replica of a sable pod  the pod is created so let's get the  service endpoint so we can issue a  request  because we only have the stable version  running we get the expected result  now let's deploy the canary version of  the application  also with one replica  because we now have both one stable pod  and one canary pod behind our service  let's issue a handful of requests to our  service  we can see that roughly we're getting  traffic split between the stable and  canary version  but wait a second this isn't how we roll  out a canary version of our app  if there are issues with that release we  don't want 50 of requests from users  seeing it  so let's scale up our stable version of  our deployment to 9 replicas in hopes  that we can get the majority of traffic  served from the stable version we'll  change the replicas value in the  deployment manifest  and apply the updated configuration  once kubernetes processes the update we  can see that we now have nine replicas  of our stable version running but still  only one replica of our canary version  in theory then we'd expect roughly 90 of  our traffic to be served by the stable  version  so let's see it  we'll run a while loop and issue a  handful of requests to the kubernetes  service  and as expected the majority of requests  are served by the stable replicas with  only a few served by the canary replica  scientific method at work  in a more real scenario we can leave  this canary running for a while to  observe the performance of the canary  release  gku's console gives us a quick way to  get information about the canary  deployment such as metrics around cpu  memory or even quick access to our logs  we can use all of this info to decide  when to promote our canary version of  our app to stable  all this said you might want a more  exact method for achieving a 9010  traffic split between stable and canary  versions of our app  remember how i said this method is  implicit  did you catch how  we are managing replicas explicitly not  incoming traffic  but this got us pretty close to what we  wanted  so where can this fall apart  well what if we had an autoscaler in  place  let's look at a scenario where we have a  horizontal pod auto scaler to scale our  stable deployment anywhere between 1 and  20 replicas  and because we're not sending much  traffic to our app we can see that the  pods begin to scale down this makes  keeping our desired ratio of stable to  canary replicas incredibly difficult  thus we want to look at an alternative  an explicit model for how we achieve a  canary deployment  we'll do this by evaluating the same app  but this time running in a cluster with  istio and antho service mesh  not familiar with what a service mesh is  check out the link in the description  and come back to this part of the video  after  so this time around we'll work with the  prod asm gke cluster which is running  istio and antho service mesh  if we take a look at our pods and  service we're back to where we started  one stable pod and one canary pod  one slight difference here is that we  are exposing the pods by an internal ip  rather than an external load bouncer  for those of you with eagle eyes you may  have noticed that each pod actually now  has two containers if we describe one of  the pods we can inspect and find that we  have a new container or a sidecar proxy  running alongside our app so using the  istio api and antho service mesh we  might actually be able to program these  proxies to get the explicit traffic  management we want  but right now as far as we know if we  were to send traffic to our service it  should still be 50 50 since there is one  replica of stable and one replica of  canary  we'll run a load generator pod in our  cluster to test this out  yep looks like our requests are pretty  evenly balanced  so what's the difference  well we can now create istio resources  to make the proxies in our pods aware of  traffic shaping that we may want to  implement we can start by creating an  istio destination rule for the app  service which allows us to make a  distinction between the two releases of  the app under our subsets field this  distinction is made by you guessed it  the specific label unique to the stable  and canary pods respectively  with this distinction made we can now  create an istio virtual service and  finally we can now make an explicit  reference under the weight field to the  percentages of traffic we want to go to  each subset stable and canary the  declarative values we use here are then  pushed out to the proxies in all of our  pods so that those proxies can perform  the explicit traffic shaping we want to  implement the canary deployment  with these istio resources created let's  send some traffic to our pods without  changing the replica counts for our  stable and canary releases  we can now see that roughly 90 of our  traffic is being served by the stable  pod which still only has one replica and  10 percent of our traffic being served  by the canary pod  all of this done explicitly  as a nice little bonus antho service  mesh provides us with monitoring  dashboards and the ability to create  slos to observe our canary deployments  with more user-centric metrics like  latency rather than just cpu utilization  or something like that  we can also view the overall service  mesh topology in the console for antho  service mesh  here we can see our explicit traffic  shaping in play with around 40 qps from  our load generator going to the stable  version of our app and 4 qps going to  the canary version of our app  and there we have it the fundamentals of  how you can perform canary deployments  in gke  i do want to leave you with a couple of  parting thoughts  one depending on your use case you may  not need to have a service mesh to  achieve explicit traffic shaping  check out the demo i put out on  multi-cluster gateways in the  description below to not only show how  you can use google cloud load balancers  to do explicit traffic shaping but also  how you can bring this stable canary  pattern across multiple clusters  and two at the end of the day whichever  approach you take explicit or implicit  it's all still yaml  the next step from here is to check out  what automation and pipeline software  you can use to orchestrate the  provisioning of all this yaml  to dig in deeper check out the resources  i've gathered for you here as well as  the links in the description below  let us know what you'd like to see from  this space next and as always thank you  for your time  you

